markup — a note for a person that a program can read too. a draft.

Written from what the notes already do (~/work/todo/*, lisp-notebook.txt),
not from a new syntax: a note that follows the rules reads the same as one
that does not. Code is a leaf of the page like prose is, not a guest in it.

the problem: three needs that look like one. a nice view (the page has it),
programmatic access (ask the notes, not scrape them), and the studio later
(new views with no change to a note). one answer: a reader that turns a note
or a folder into the list, saved as json. the text is the surface, the list
is the truth, a view is a renderer over it (renderer: node, renderer, page).
built: medium/page/list reads a note, a folder or an html page to the list,
lisp or --json. next: page/todo reads through it; the round trip.

├── tree     a heading is a node, nesting is depth
├── head     key: value lines on top
├── prose    a paragraph, a list, a step
├── code     indented, kept as typed, and it is a leaf with a name
├── refs     what a bare path or #id becomes
└── lisp     how it maps to the page's own form


## rules

  1  heading   "# x" or "## x": a node, depth is the number of #. a line
               that is only "**x:**" is one too, a level under the last
               "#". id is the slug, its address: page.html#id
  2  head      the first lines, "key: value", until a blank: title, state,
               after, tags. what the lens reads today, unchanged
  3  prose     lines to a blank are a paragraph. "- " is a list, nested by
               indent. "- [ ]" and "- [x]" are steps: the node's pill is
               2/5 done
  4  code      a block indented 2+ after a blank, or fenced, is code: kept
               as typed, columns and all, copy beside it. the line before it,
               if it ends in ":", is its caption. "$ cmd" is a command
  5  rows      "name  value", two spaces or more, is a row: a table with no
               bars. "|" tables still work
  6  refs      path, path:line, path:/pattern/ and #id are links. inline
               `x` is code. nothing else is inline: no bold, no italics

  rule 1 is the only one that makes nodes. the rest are leaves. so the
  engine does not change: it already takes nodes, and a leaf is html.


## code first

  a block is a value, not a picture of one: it has a caption, a kind, and
  later a name. that is what lets the page do something with it.

  now      kept as typed, copy beside it
  later    "name.py" as the caption: a link to the file's own line
  later    a block that is data (rows, json) can be checked or sorted
  later    a lisp block is evaluated by the page: (note ...) is a note

  nothing here asks the writer to leave plain text. every later row is
  opt-in by a caption.


## as the page's own form

  the same note, as lisp: a tree of named parts.

    (node "Databases"
      (para "An index speeds up queries")
      (node "Transactions"
        (para "A transaction is all-or-nothing")))

  and as these rules:

    # Databases
    An index speeds up queries

    ## Transactions
    A transaction is all-or-nothing

  one to one. the text is what you write; the list is what a program
  holds. either way the page gets a details#id per node.


## tried on

  lisp-notebook.txt   "pollen - ..." line is the title; each "**x:**" is a
                      node, 6 of them; "- " lists stay; the (section ...)
                      and {(define ...)} blocks are code, as they are
  01-databricks       its tree (├──) is a code block, its "## case" a node,
                      "rule / message / payload" three rows
  18-jivasup66        "## Problem" and "## Branch / pin map" nodes; the
                      table is a "|" table; "Chain: a → b" a prose line
                      with three refs

  fits with no edit to the notes. what does not: columns hand-aligned
  without a blank above them (rule 4 needs the blank).


## found, on 01-databricks (viz2)

  the real notes (readme, next) do not use "#". a bare line with lines
  indented under it is a node; "name  text" with lines under it is a row
  that goes on. indent is depth: the list's own shape. so:

  - rule 1 gains a second form: a line with deeper lines under it is a node
  - rule 4 conflicts: indent can no longer mean code. code is fenced, or a
    file with a shebang, or a block whose caption says so
  - a folder is a node too: its files are the children, and a file that is
    a note opens into its own. one list for disk and text


